# Pulsar — Cline working rules (Milestone 2)

1. SPEC.md is the source of truth. docs/SPEC.md governs all design
   decisions; do not deviate from it without asking the user first.
2. Work one milestone at a time (SPEC section 13). Each milestone must pass
   its exit test before the next one starts.
3. All builds run inside the Distrobox container "juce" (Ubuntu 22.04).
   The terminal is on the SteamOS host, so wrap every build command as:
   distrobox enter juce -- <command>
4. After every code change, run the build and fix errors before continuing.
5. Real-time rules (SPEC section 11): on the audio thread, no allocation,
   locks, logging, I/O, exceptions, or capturing std::function. Threads
   communicate through SPSC queues and atomics only. No -ffast-math on
   validation, safety or guard code. NaN/Inf checks use bit-pattern tests,
   not std::isnan.
6. Dependencies must be permissive (MIT/BSD/zlib). Never add LGPL/GPL
   dependencies other than JUCE itself. Keep THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md up to
   date as dependencies are added.
7. Never hardcode API keys or write them to any file in the repo. The key
   lives in the settings panel / VIBESYNTH_API_KEY env var only (SPEC 6.5).
8. Do not invent JUCE APIs. If unsure about a JUCE signature or behaviour,
   check the JUCE source in the build's CPM cache (build/**/_deps/) before
   writing code.
9. Naming (SPEC section 1): everything a user sees is **Pulsar** -- the plugin
   name in the DAW, the `.vst3` bundle, the Standalone app, window titles,
   release zips, the app-data folder and the README. "VibeSynth" is the
   internal codename and may appear only in code: the CMake target, class
   names, `vibesynth_cli` and the `VIBESYNTH_API_KEY` env var.
10. A directory on an include path must not contain extensionless files
    (e.g. VERSION, LICENSE) or any file whose name, ignoring case and
    extension, matches a standard header (version, limits, string, etc.):
    macOS and Windows are case-insensitive, so `#include <version>` can pick
    such a file up. .cpp files next to headers are fine.
